A||||35||the need for speed||is there a kit for buying polyurethane suspension bushings||||1||
Z||000000||the need for speed||10-24-2000||12:05 PM||||if i want to put polyurethane suspension bushings on my pre 88, what kit will i need and how much do you think it will coast?I am looking at suspension.com s web page, i see a front control arm and a rear control arm is this all i need or do these two kits cover what is needed? i already have a poly dog bone and a 1 inch front sway bar with poly so i need to get poly for adding a rear swaybar.i was going to get an alinement but should i wait to put the poly on or does it matter meaning do i need to have the car alined after putting on the bushings?   thx for the help!!!!!<BR>how fast is suspension . com in shiping there stuff<BR>         <P><p>[This message has been edited by the need for speed (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-24-2000||12:42 PM||||The Fiero Store sells the bushing kits.  Check out their web site. <P>They have all the bushings but when I totaled up the $ layout for all of them, its like $217.   You need to check the store out to see which one you need.  <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com</A>     Is this spam?<P><p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||1FST2M6||10-24-2000||01:17 PM||||poly bushings # 7-304, 7-502 and 7-207 call Nopi or go to <A HREF="http://www.nopi.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nopi.com</A>  and save yourself $60. rear cradle mounts are $40, the 304 18.87, and the 207s are 32.37!! the sway bars you can go to the nopi site or contace prothane and give them your applocation and give theri part number to a Nopi employee and save at least 30% over the fiero store... no i don't work their... i just saw the fiero store prices and know that they are charging 2.5* what they actually pay for it!<P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||10-24-2000||02:09 PM||||i just talked with NOPI... the part numbers are correct, here is a description of whats what:<BR>7-304 is the Rear Control Arm kit, $18.87<BR>7-502 Cradle mount's, $32.37<BR>7-207 Front Control Arm kit, $32.37<P>The salseperson seemed a little short with me... (go figure, there mostly rice boys toys anyway).<P>He did not know delivery, as they had to order them direct from the mfg., or quantity... i.e.: complete set for both sides, or what.<P>How are they for delivery?  This is much cheaper than Prothane!  Does it include detailed intructions?<P>Thanks!<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||10-24-2000||02:30 PM||||It looks like Nopi's prices are even lower than suspension.com<P>When I put poly on my Formula, I checked with Nopi. Only person on the other end was some moron who couldn't even spell "Pontiac" for the computer, let along understand what a suspension bushing was. You must have gotten lucky, and talked to someone who knew what they were doing. There is a thread from a few months back detailing where I bought my bushings, the headaches I encountered, and describes the procedure for removing/installing(plus it describes my encounter with Nopi(sorry 1FAST, I was just not impressed with them at all)). I think the thread title was "suspension progress", should be quite helpful, maybe I'll dig it up for you.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||10-24-2000||02:36 PM||||Found it: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005387.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005387.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||10-24-2000||04:30 PM||||most of the people at NOPI are douche bags yes.. but if you give them a part number you'll be ok. deliver is usually pretty good 5 working days. and shipping will be about $5-$10. Ups ground or 3day is about $5 more. if you call NOPI call 877-700-6674 ask for Chris Ellingwood(Smokin Boy)(Turbo ZC 89 CRX Si w/nitrous) or Danny (don't know his last name) they JUST saw my new Oange car today.. and they both have been scared passengers in my Gold SE. <P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BAM-BAM||10-24-2000||05:22 PM||||  Try this link, I went there and they were great!<P> <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A>  <P> They'll have just wnat you need and at a fair price.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Guages And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||12
Z||000008||theogre||10-24-2000||07:53 PM||||Haven't delt with NOPI, can't say anything one way or the other about them service wise. They might be cheaper but if they say they don't have it in stock you have to decide if you're willing to wait for them to get it in then ship to you.<P>Have delt with <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/" TARGET=_blank>Suspension Restoration Parts Co.</A> (suspension.com)and was more than happy with him. He keeps pretty much everything he sells in stock and can ship it however you want it. I had my cradel set shipped ups ground and it took about 4-5 days from him to DE. (I think he's in CA someplace.)||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||10-24-2000||08:30 PM||||I have to agree with Ogre. Don't have any personal experience with anything except suspension.com which has worked out great so far. They were the cheapest when I looked around a year ago, but maybe not now.<P>Need FUR speed, get the alignment AFTER the poly bushing install. You have to split the ball joints off the spindle to remove the control arms. It's possible one will get damaged in the process, or you might find they have a little wear and decide to replace the ball joints. Ditto with the tie rod ends. You'll need to have it realigned if those parts get replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||10-25-2000||07:56 AM||||Fiero56... I had read that thread b4.  In the end, did you have decent success with the propane torch?  How much time, from start to finish did it take for you to finish the bushing job?<P>Have you done the rear yet?  It seems like that may be the easiest to do.  I may start with that then do the front over xmas.<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||10-25-2000||11:54 AM||||Fiero56, Read your previous thread. Good Job! Yes I like the set-up also. I did my suspension over last winter (KYB, Eibach, and poly). The only thing I haven't done yet was the cradle bushings. Being an Auto trans the bolts most likely haven't moved in 15 years. So I'll try them this winter. ||||reg||14
Z||000012||fiero56||10-25-2000||12:15 PM||||I've been trying to post all day. My password was changed???<P>I haven't replaced the rear bushings yet, school started, and this car is my daily driver. When I have vacation I will be replacing the rear. <P>I am not familiar with the pre-'88 suspension, but from what I have seen, it looks like replacing the rear bushings shuold be cake.<P>The front UCA bushings are by far the worst you'll encounter. I never got the bushgins to just pop out while using propane, I had to melt, and scrape, and melt... Took 30 minutes+ for each UCA bushing(x4). <P>Another suggestion is to go heavy on the bushing grease. I used it <B>very</B>sparingly on the passneger side, and heavily on the driver side since I had so much left. The passenger side will squeak when it is cold(40 and below), but the drivers side is silent. The difference from the old rubber is amazing, and I will definitely be putting poly on my new SE if I ever get it going.<P>To remove the LCA bushings, I drilled about 8 holes in the rubber, stuck a phillips scrwedriver in, and ripped up the bushing into chuncks and let the pieced fall out. Then, used a wire wheel on a drill to clean out the bushing shell. The UCA is the worst.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pffsig1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||10-25-2000||01:44 PM||||I just ordered the bushings from NOPI... boy what a bunch of <B>Snots!</B><P>Anyway, total charge (with special order charge & shipping) was $100!<P>A lot cheaper than anywhere else.<P>Delivery is quoted 7-10 working days... I dont know if I can be without my car for 3 week? But to save $50-100, I guess I can deal with it.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||the need for speed||10-25-2000||02:59 PM||||fiero store<P> $217 for kit<P>suspension.com<P>front control arm bushing set $54.00<BR>rear control arm bushing set $30.00<BR>front sway bar bushings $14.00<BR>front sway bar endlink set $15.00<P>nopi<P>front control arm kit $32.37<BR>rear control arm kit $18.87   WOW<P>i think as far as engine cradle mounts go i hear that the people on the forum are going aluminum<BR>does any one know the price and part number for the swaybar endlinks and bushings from nopi??? they defiantly sound like the way to go so far<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Pontiaddict||10-25-2000||04:18 PM||||Can you use aluminum without pressing the outer bushing sleeves out? <P>I overpaid for my suspension parts because I'm lazy and wanted to get all my stuff from one place.<P>What's the best way to get the rear cradle bolts out without breaking them. (being that they are rusted in)||||reg||1
Z||000016||bHooper||10-25-2000||05:31 PM||||Speeking of "press"... I was looking through the JC Whitney Winter special catalog and came across a <B>Bushing Driver Set</B>!<P>anyone ever use one of these?  Will it do the job of removing and installing the bushings?<P>for $12.39+s/h  removal, alone would be satisfactory!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000017||batboy||10-25-2000||08:17 PM||||I used the torch method on my control arm bushings and had no trouble at all. The brand of poly bushings sold by suspension.com is Prothane, an established reputable company. I wonder what brand the cheapies are from NOPI? Maybe better, or maybe worse? Hmmm.||||reg||1
Z||000018||webbee||10-25-2000||08:35 PM||||Hoop, please update us on brand ang quality. TIA||||reg||1
Z||000019||fiero56||10-25-2000||10:21 PM||||From what I have heard, Prothane is the only company that makes poly for the Fiero.<P>I would be very interested to know that if what Nopi shipped out was actually a bushing set, and not a sticker set or other. I had to spell Pontiac twice, explain that Fiero was the model name, and the dumba$$ had the nuts to say he didn't think they carried suspension parts. Not my kind of company, but to each his own. I ordered from <BR> <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com</A> <P>The guy on the phone knew exactly what I was looking for, knew how to spell, had heard of the Fiero, and I received my bushings in two days(I think, may have been next day). I will be ordering the rear bushings for mine from him.||||reg||1
Z||000020||bHooper||10-26-2000||06:52 AM||||i gave them part numbers, thanks Travis, and didn't have to deal with spelling anything other than my name and address.  from what i can tell, they get the parts drop shipped from a company that might be the parent co. to prothane, or vice versa!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000021||1FST2M6||10-26-2000||08:18 AM||||sure anytime i can help! and for the uninformed Prothane is a subsidiary(sp) of Dee-Engineering (yeah the old Bug people) and they are the ONLY company that makes full suspension sets for our cars.. so humm wonder who makes them.. the part numbers are the same as Fiero Store, Prothane, cyberauto.com hmm... you can try the pointing an laughing all you want but i STILL paid less for the SAME parts you got.  the bushing press is a must for the cradle! you can rent them at most local auto parts stores. for like $25 but when you return it you'll get $20 back.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> easyest way i've found was to take the part off lay a Propane torch down next to the bushing till its WELL lit and move to the next one... do this on WET concrete or on a trash sheet of metal it'll wash off the wet concrete a borrowed men working sign works well...     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  expect LOTS of black smoke and staring neighbors!<P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg">     <BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||bHooper||11-07-2000||07:42 AM||||i thought everyone would be interested to know, that i received my "cheap" poly bushings, yesterday.<P>these are the <B>EXACT</B> same bushings that you can order from Suspension Techniques/Prothane, or the Fiero Store!<P>i will be installing these this week, and hope to have my car back on the road by mid next week.<P>one question... is it ok to substitute silicone grease for teflon grease, when installing the bushings?<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000023||fieroshop||11-07-2000||11:56 AM||||We also carry the poly bushings here at the Fiero Shop.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||1FST2M6||11-07-2000||02:31 PM||||if you don't have the CRAZY sticky silicone grease use molybdenym grease. Glad to hear you got your parts..     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  what color black? you get them all? how much are they from the fiero shop?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mouse.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstsig1.jpg">    <BR> <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||bHooper||11-07-2000||02:38 PM||||Yeah, I got them all.<P>Red<P>I bought Permatex, teflon based grease, while at lunch.  Hopefully that will do it!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000026||1FST2M6||11-08-2000||10:42 AM||||that'll do! just make sure you do both load bearing sides!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> COOL red! they make, yellow, pink, blue and black too!!   ||||reg||1
Z||000027||bHooper||11-08-2000||11:13 AM||||...both load bearing sides?  are you talking about the inner and outer sleaves?<P>i salved the hell out of the outer bushing sleaves... need to do the inner sleave's them i'm done, except for the cradle.  i have to get the front suspension on b4 i can do that.<P>the most difficult part of installing the bushings, so far, were getting them in the upper control arms... couldnt get my clamp on them, so i took them to a machine shop and they pressed them in... at No charge!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Glad to get something for free, on this job!<P>When all is said and done, the only thing's that I will not have replaced on the suspension, will be the rear inner tie rod ends (plan to do RCC bump steer kit in summer) and the half shafts (will replace when 3.4 l is added next winter)<P>It should ride like a dream!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000028||1FST2M6||11-08-2000||11:24 AM||||yeah it' moves too! you don't want ANYTHING that moves not lubed.. it'll wear faster and squeek. i'm sure it'll ride SWEET! let us know! ||||reg||1
Z||000029||bHooper||11-08-2000||12:48 PM||||oh yeah, one other thing that i cant change, for a bit, are the tires.  i have a 6 y/o set of toyo 600+4 on the front (plent of tread, a little dry rot showing... the car did sit for 4 years) and i had to put a pair of used tires on the rear several weeks ago when i developed 2 flats.  those are General 215/65's.  <B>i really hope they dont cause any rubbing isues</B>, now that i have added Eibachs!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000030||william_thorniii||11-09-2000||05:56 PM||||Friends,<P>I am confused once again.  For those of you that don't know, I am in the middle of a project in which I am mating the rear suspension from an 88 model year to my 86 SE.  The 86 came with poly front sway bar bushings when I bought it.  I am trying to prepare for my call to nopi and thus want my information straight.  <P>So far the numbers that I know I need are as follows:<BR>7-207 Front Control Arm Kit<BR>7-501 Dog Bone Bushings<BR>5-9409 Rear End Link Kit<BR>7-305 88 Trailing Link Kit<P>The numbers that I am slightly confused are as follows:<BR>9-5127 Front Sway Bar Bushings 23mm (from Suspension.com)<BR>51132 Front Sway Bar Bushings (from Fiero Store)<BR>9-13101 Tie Rod End Boot (from Suspension.com...http://www.suspension.com/pontiac.htm#fiero)<P>The questions I have are as follows:<P>1) What is the correct front sway bar bushing number?  The one from the Fiero Store or Suspension.com?<P>2) Are the Tie Rod End Boots really available?  I understood that there were not any around?  <P>3) I was under the impression that there were engine and transmission mounts available for our powertrains.  Is this not the case?  If not, please inform me as to where I can find information on their part numbers. <P>4) While I have your attention... I need more front swaybar.  What options do I have?<P>As usual any and all help is greatly appreciated.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000031||bHooper||11-09-2000||07:23 PM||||WtIII... it appears that the bushing numbers are the same for NOPI as everything else.<P>as for the tranny/engine, if you want poly... i believe Chris West offers poly mounts.  othersise, check out partsamerica.com  they have excellent prices on the rubber stuff!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/alien.gif">  <BR>We are not alone!||||reg||1
Z||000032||william_thorniii||11-10-2000||08:32 AM||||hoop,<P>That is exactly what I had heard before, and why I am so perplexed by the following:<P>"9-5127 Front Sway Bar Bushings 23mm (from Suspension.com)<BR>51132 Front Sway Bar Bushings (from Fiero Store)"<P>The descriptions are the same, but the part numbers are different.  I just don't want to pay shipping twice if it isn't the right part.  <P>Thanks Again,<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000033||bHooper||11-10-2000||09:17 AM||||Sorry, missunderstood your question.<P>It appears that the Fiero Store has their own part numbers, for obvious reasons.<P>The Suspension tech part numbers seem to match the NOPI ones, as NOPI gets their parts drop shipped direct from Dee Engineering/Prothane.<P>It took about 10 working days for me to get my parts, but I saved $50-70!<P>Hope this helps clear some of the confusion... if not, maybe Travis (1fst2m6) can help.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/alien.gif">  <BR>We are not alone!||||reg||1
Z||000034||william_thorniii||11-10-2000||09:43 AM||||OK... I understand now... thanks hoop||||reg||14
